[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: The deterministic scenario suite leaves large parts of the contracted public behavior of VersionEngine, PackageRepository, and DependencySolver unverified. Missing are scenarios for the low-level specification's range grammar: whitespace between an operator and its target, each contracted basic comparator form with boundary-equality cases, caret expansion at the first non-zero component for major, minor, and patch boundaries, tilde expansion to the next minor boundary, wildcard components normalized into paired inequalities, hyphen ranges, and union alternatives, plus range targets carrying prerelease or build segments. Missing are VersionEngine.satisfies postconditions for multi-comparator conjunction, disjunction across union clauses, and prerelease filtering when include_prerelease is False versus direct precedence evaluation when it is True, including ranges that explicitly target a prerelease sharing the candidate's numeric components. Missing are compare_precedence assertions for prerelease ordering: numeric versus string identifiers, unequal prerelease sequence lengths, and normal-over-prerelease ranking with identical numeric components. Missing are repository postconditions for descending precedence ordering and shallow-copy retrieval across multiple releases of one package, and DependencySolver.resolve postconditions for transitive dependency constraint merging, fewest-candidate selection, descending candidate exploration, backtracking to an alternative release when a candidate's constraints prove unsatisfiable, and derivation-step recording.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: Expand the deterministic scenario suite: operator-whitespace and every basic comparator boundary, caret major/minor/patch, tilde, wildcard, hyphen, union and multi-comparator ranges, prerelease/build range targets, prerelease filtering vs direct precedence, prerelease ordering (numeric vs string, sequence length, normal-over-prerelease), descending repository ordering with shallow-copy retrieval, and transitive/backtracking resolution with derivation steps.
[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_pretense.py: The interface state machine's require_arg domains and satisfies model contradict low/semver_solver.pyi. compare_precedence gates v1 and v2 with Domain.in_set(self.state.known_versions) (lines 231-232), yet the contract accepts any two Version arguments and only mandates negative/zero/positive precedence results, so freshly constructed contract-valid Versions are wrongly rejected. satisfies (line 262) gates version with Domain.in_set(self.state.known_versions) and range_constraint with Domain.in_set(self.state.known_ranges), but the contract accepts any Version and any VersionRange, so arbitrary valid candidates are rejected before evaluation. satisfies also models its return as include_prerelease or not bool(version.prerelease), ignoring range_constraint entirely, so the mandated comparator, caret, tilde, wildcard, hyphen, union, equality and boundary postconditions can never be observed regardless of the version passed. parse_range (line 246) applies Domain.regex(_RANGE_PATTERN) whose _RANGE_VERSION_TEXT omits build metadata, so contract-valid targets such as '>=1.2.3+build.5' fail domain validation even though the contract's parse_range must parse comparator targets and SemVer 2.0.0 permits build metadata. register_package (line 276) gates release with Domain.in_set(self.state.releases), though the contract accepts any PackageRelease. Open these domains (Domain.type or open domains with preferred samples for fuzz guidance) and replace the prerelease-presence stub with reference precedence evaluation over the parsed comparator clauses so fresh contract-valid arguments reach the modeled operation and the modelled outcome matches the specified postconditions.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_pretense.py: Open interface domains to contract-valid arguments: compare_precedence accepts any Version, satisfies accepts any Version and syntactically valid VersionRange, and register_package accepts any PackageRelease, with state pools retained as preferred fuzz samples. Replace the prerelease-presence stub with real range evaluation over parsed comparator clauses (basic comparators, caret/tilde expansion, wildcard normalization, hyphen ranges, union disjunction, build-ignoring precedence, and prerelease targeting).
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: Synchronize with the opened interface domains: resolve scenarios now seed their root dependency pool explicitly instead of relying on register_package bookkeeping, keeping scenario validation aligned with the contract-accepting model domains.
[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_pretense.py: The range model in this interface state machine does not satisfy low/semver_solver.pyi: the domain it admits is broader than the syntax its evaluator implements. In satisfies (line 393 assert ret is result) the modeled result disagrees with the mandated engine result for the minimal admitted counterexample version=Version(0,0,0), range text='0.0.0\x85-\x850.0.1' (NEL is Unicode whitespace matched by \s in _RANGE_PATTERN), include_prerelease=False: _clause_comparators (lines 188-207) detects hyphen ranges only via the literal ASCII substring ' - ', so a contract-valid whitespace-delimited hyphen range whose separators are other whitespace characters is tokenized into ['0.0.0','-','0.0.1'] and the standalone '-' is treated as a comparator whose target _parse_version_text (lines 88-107) silently coerces malformed text to Version(0,0,0) instead of gating it, yielding the conjunction ==0.0.0 ==0.0.0 ==0.0.1 and False, whereas the contract requires hyphen ranges M1.m1.p1 - M2.m2.p2 to denote an inclusive interval so 0.0.0 must satisfy it; the model must evaluate any whitespace form its own parse_range/satisfies domains admit (restrict the domain to ASCII whitespace or make hyphen/comparator splitting whitespace-agnostic) and must never silently coerce unparsable version tokens. Separately, _wildcard_comparators (lines 155-165) performs int(parts[0]) before the len(parts)==1 return, so the contract-supported major-only wildcards 'X', 'x' and '*' raise ValueError (minimal counterexample version=Version(0,0,0), range text='=X', include_prerelease=False), and two-component wildcards such as '1.x.x'/'1.*.*' reach int(parts[1]) with a non-numeric token; the contract parse_range must support wildcards *, x, X, M.x, M.*, M.m.x, M.m.*, so the model must normalize a wildcard component to its unbounded comparator set (an entirely-wildcard range matching all versions) instead of int-converting wildcard tokens. These model defects must be fixed so the admitted domain and the modeled evaluation agree with the contract's mandated range semantics, and the return comparison should be a value comparison against the expected model outcome rather than identity on the engine result.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_pretense.py: Make the range evaluator whitespace-agnostic to match the admitted domain: hyphen separators are detected from Unicode-whitespace tokens, unparsable version tokens raise instead of coercing to 0.0.0, bare/major/two-component wildcards normalize without int-converting wildcard tokens, and satisfies compares its return by value against the expected model outcome.
