[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: The deterministic scenario suite still leaves two contracted behaviors unverified. For VersionEngine.compare_precedence, no scenario covers prerelease segment ordering when the segments compared at a position are both numeric but differ in integer value, when a shared numeric prefix ties and comparison continues into a later segment, or when both segments are alphanumeric and ordering follows ASCII code-point order; the existing scenarios only exercise numeric-versus-alphanumeric ranking, unequal sequence lengths, and normal-versus-prerelease presence. For DependencySolver.resolve, no scenario covers the postcondition that when the highest-precedence candidate introduces a transitive requirement that no registered release can satisfy, including a requirement on a package entirely absent from the repository, the search records the dead end and retreats to the next lower-precedence candidate, that constraints introduced by the abandoned candidate no longer constrain the alternative, and that the resulting outcome reports the selected versions and derivation steps of the successful assignment.
[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/lib/semver_solver_impl.py: The residual uncovered statements of this unit are restricted to code that conforming Pretense scenarios cannot exercise, in two categories. First, input-rejection and contract-error handling: the invalid-range and invalid-version error value constructions, every guard that returns without a clause or raises for malformed range syntax (non-string, empty or whitespace-only text, comma-separated comparators, empty union clauses, dangling operators, bare partial versions lacking wildcards, partial caret, tilde and relational targets, non-trailing wildcard groups, and wildcard targets carrying prerelease or build metadata), the package-not-found raise, and the resolution-conflict explanation assembly and raise. Second, branch semantics outside the low-level contract closure: wildcard-only and negation-combined wildcard normalizations, caret, tilde and relational comparators applied to wildcard or component-less targets, and the equal-length prerelease ranking return, which is dead because identical prerelease tuples are already resolved earlier under the precedence rules. Restructure to eliminate the dead statements, or mark each contract-error and unreachable branch with unreachable() or a no-cover assumption annotation justified by the caller-enforced precondition or impossible branch, leaving only the contracted range algebra, precedence, repository and resolution statements as the measured surface.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: Add missing scenarios: prerelease precedence for differing numeric segment values, continued comparison after a shared numeric prefix, and ASCII code-point ordering of alphanumeric identifiers; plus resolution retreating from the highest-precedence candidate whose transitive requirements reference an absent package and an unsatisfiable range, verifying the abandoned constraints do not bind the alternative and the successful assignment reports versions and derivation steps.
