[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: Two contracted resolution behaviors still lack deterministic scenario coverage. First, no scenario exercises the zero-candidate treatment of a package absent from the repository inside the search itself: the existing retreat scenario makes the abandoned candidate unsatisfiable through a conflicting requirement on an already-chosen release, so the absent requirement is never the failing facet; a scenario is needed in which the only reason the highest-precedence candidate's subtree is unsatisfiable is a transitive requirement on a package absent from the repository, and the search thereby retreats to the lower-precedence candidate and resolves successfully with the absent package excluded from the selected versions and derivation steps. Second, no scenario covers exhaustion of every candidate release of an intermediate package: each candidate branch dead-ends, so the search retreats a further level before a lower-precedence root candidate resolves successfully; the reported versions and derivation steps of that successful assignment are the contract-mandated postconditions needing assertion.
[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/lib/semver_solver_impl.py: The single uncovered statement in this unit, at line 104, is the continuation taken when two pre-release identifiers at the same position are both numeric and equal as integers. That branch is a defensive check on a precondition the SemVer 2.0.0 record contract already guarantees: numeric pre-release identifiers carry no leading zeroes, so two numerically equal numeric identifiers are necessarily textually identical and are already continued by the deliberately ordered textual-equality check preceding it. The branch is therefore reachable only for Version records that do not conform to the grammar the immutable record contract describes, which conforming scenarios cannot construct. Under the coverage methodology, prune the redundant continuation, or annotate it with a no-cover assumption stating that equal numeric identifiers are already handled by the textual comparison above it, so that only contracted precedence statements remain measured.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/lib/semver_solver_impl.py: Resolve remaining coverage feedback at compare_precedence: pruned the redundant 'if left_number == right_number: continue' continuation inside the numeric prerelease-segment branch. SemVer 2.0.0 numeric identifiers carry no leading zeroes, so numerically equal identifiers are necessarily textually identical and are already continued by the preceding textual-equality check; the removed branch was reachable only for non-conforming Version records. Numeric precedence ordering (numeric below string, integer ordering, longer-sequence-higher tail) is unchanged for all grammatical versions.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: Add two resolution scenarios: (1) the highest-precedence candidate's only dead end is a transitive requirement on a package absent from the repository, so the search retreats to the lower candidate with the absent package absent from selected versions and derivation steps; (2) every candidate release of an intermediate package dead-ends on absent packages, exhausting that level before the search retreats to a lower-precedence root candidate and reports the successful assignment's versions and derivation steps.
